--TEST--
`Core\Topic::publish` answers how many subscribers it reached, and is the one member of the class that needs no connection of its own — ADR 0083 § 4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 4's second row asks nothing about the program calling it. `subscribe` and
// `unsubscribe` refuse anything that is not a connection, because a
// subscription with no queue behind it is an entry nothing could ever drain --
// but a publish is a value handed to whoever joined, and the commonest reason
// to have a bus at all is an ordinary request telling the connections that
// something changed. This script is no kind of connection, and it may speak.
echo Core\Topic::publish("room:lobby", "hello"), "\n";

// What it answers is the count delivered to, so a topic nobody joined is a
// topic with nobody on it rather than an error: a publisher learns how many
// were reached, never whether it should have bothered.
echo Core\Topic::publish("room:lobby", "again"), "\n";
echo Core\Topic::publish("room:never-mentioned", 42), "\n";

// It is a `uint`, so it counts like one rather than reading like one.
var $reached = Core\Topic::publish("room:lobby", 42);
echo $reached + 1, "\n";

// Publishing does not join anything. A name reaches the bus's table when a
// connection subscribes to it and at no other moment, so a hundred publishes
// to one name leave it exactly as empty as they found it.
echo Core\Topic::publish("room:lobby", null), "\n";
--EXPECT--
0
0
0
1
0
